Thread VP9 software decoding by rows on OHOS, and say how long frames take.

A phone here decodes 10-bit VP9 in software: it has no VP9 hardware decoder,
and unlike mobile Android this port builds libvpx with high bit depth, so
YouTube serves it 1080p60 HDR (itag 335). On a Mate 70 Pro+ one such video
dropped 1% of its frames and another 30%, with the decode thread about half
busy on both and libvpx's other threads idle: libvpx threads across tile
columns only, and a frame that ran long on the one busy thread missed its
16.7 ms and was dropped.

On OHOS the VP9 decoder asks libvpx to thread by rows as well
(VP9D_SET_ROW_MT), which spreads a frame with a single tile row over the
decoder's threads however many columns it has -- libvpx threads nothing
else -- and logs its thread count once and, every 300 frames,
the average and longest vpx_codec_decode time and how many frames went over
16.7 ms ("OHOS vpx decode"). YouTube's 1080p60 HDR turned out to have
more than one tile row: still one busy thread, 9-10 ms a frame on average
and runs of 20-80 ms, which ohos-video-queue-depth.patch is for.

Those times are wall time, and even 426x240 took 3-7 ms a frame: the decode
thread was runnable but not running about half the time. HarmonyOS
schedules by QoS level rather than by the nice value base sets, so the
decoding thread is raised to QOS_USER_INTERACTIVE for the length of each
decode (OH_QoS_SetThreadQoS, from libqos.so opened at run time) and put back
after, being a thread pool thread. Whether the raise is available is logged
once.

Generated against Chromium 154.0.8037.51.

Apply with:
  git -C /path/to/chromium/src apply /path/to/patches/ohos-vp9-row-threads.patch

